{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA — reproduced, root-caused, attributed (Phase 1 of /rebar-debug)\n\n### 1. Reproduced (dynamic claim, observed at runtime — not read from source)\n\nTwo-level closed chain (epic parent + task child, both `closed`), then\n`rebar.transition(child, \"closed\", \"in_progress\")` against a real store:\n\n```\nRESULT: {'ticket_id': '33c6-e9d8-5924-47ae', 'from': 'closed', 'to': 'in_progress', 'newly_unblocked': []}\nOBSERVED child=in_progress parent=closed\n```\n\nExit 0, no error, no cascade. The report is confirmed exactly as written.\n\n### 2. Proven root cause (a changeable artifact)\n\n`_CASCADING_EDGES` in `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:408-411` holds exactly two\nentries — `(\"open\",\"in_progress\")` and `(\"closed\",\"open\")`. `_cascade_parent_first`\nlooks the child's `(current, target)` edge up in that table and **returns immediately\nwhen the lookup misses** (`parent_status = _CASCADING_EDGES.get(...)`; `if parent_status\nis None: return`). `(\"closed\",\"in_progress\")` is not a key, so the cascade is a no-op on\nthat edge and the child moves alone. Nothing else guards the direction: the\nunresolved-children guard only refuses *closing* a parent over non-closed children; it\nsays nothing about reactivating a child under a closed parent.\n\nDiscriminating evidence: on the SAME store, the same two-level closed chain, the\n`closed -> open` edge (`reopen`) **does** cascade (`parent=open`), while\n`closed -> in_progress` does not. Same helper, same store, same fixture — the only\nvariable is the table lookup. That rules out \"parent resolution is broken\",\n\"the fixture parent link is absent\", and \"the cascade is globally disabled\".\n\n### 3. The resulting state IS invalid — four citations\n\n- `docs/concurrency.md` §I4a, first sentence: **\"A child never runs ahead of its parent\n  in the lifecycle.\"** The general design invariant. An `in_progress` child under a\n  `closed` parent is the maximal violation of it.\n- `docs/concurrency.md` §I4a: \"...without the cascade a `reopen` left the parent closed —\n  **an invalid closed-parent-with-open-child state**\". The doc names this state class\n  invalid in so many words; `in_progress` is strictly further ahead than `open`.\n- `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:437` (`reopen` docstring): \"so a reopen can never leave a\n  **closed parent holding a non-closed child**.\" `non-closed` includes `in_progress`.\n- The close-path unresolved-children guard refuses to *create* this state from the\n  closing direction, so the store already treats it as one it must not compose.\n\nSo this is a `defect`, not `intended-behavior`. The deferral recorded on this ticket is\na scope decision about *when* to fix it, not an authority that the state is acceptable —\nand a392's own landing comment calls the same state \"precisely the invalid state this\nbug exists to prevent.\"\n\n### 4. What `reopen` does by comparison\n\n`reopen` is a thin delegation to `transition(id, \"closed\", \"open\")`\n(`_lib_writes.py:444`), and that edge **is** in the table, so the sanctioned\nclosed-to-active path cascades: closed parent is reopened first, recursively, fail-fast,\nwith the TOCTOU benign-race recheck. Verified live on the same fixture. The direct\n`closed -> in_progress` edge is therefore the **outlier beside an already-closed hole**,\nnot a missing feature — the machinery, the helper, the error shape, the cycle guard and\nthe race policy all already exist and are exercised by the neighbouring edge.\n\n### 5. Restoration, not widening (the operator's scope rule)\n\n`rebar transition <child> closed in_progress` **already succeeds today** — reproduced\nabove at exit 0. The fix does not make a previously-refused transition succeed; it does\nnot add an entry point, a CLI verb, a user-visible artifact, or a new mutating call. It\nadds one key to an existing table so an already-permitted edge also moves the parent.\n\nIf anything the change **narrows**: the cascaded parent runs its own start-work\nplan-review gate, so some child reactivations that succeed today will be refused with an\nerror naming the parent — exactly as the `open -> in_progress` edge has behaved since\n#49 (`docs/concurrency.md` §I4a \"Gate interaction\"). Enforcing a documented invariant on\nan already-permitted edge is restoration; permitting a refused edge would have been\nwidening, and that is not what this is.\n\n### 6. Defect origin — `caused_by` a392-f630-7b25-4b14 (cranial-sulfur-peafowl)\n\nAttributed by **scope-of-work** (this is an omission, so no commit \"broke\" it), and the\nblame trail agrees rather than diverging:\n\n- `git log -S 'never runs ahead of its parent' -- docs/concurrency.md` -> **one** commit:\n  `ed864a9726` (a392). The *general* invariant sentence was authored there. The prior\n  I4a text (`edece68877`, task 9788, 2026-06-26) was narrowly scoped — \"a descendant is\n  never moved into progress while an ancestor is left merely `open`\" — which the\n  `closed` parent case does not violate. So the doc-vs-code divergence did not exist\n  before 2026-08-19.\n- `git log -L 396,412:src/rebar/_commands/transition.py` -> the `_CASCADING_EDGES` table\n  and its \"There are exactly two cascading edges\" enumeration are both `ed864a9726`.\n- `_lib_writes.py:437`'s \"can never leave a closed parent holding a non-closed child\"\n  is also `ed864a9726`.\n- **Date test passes**: the implicated lines do NOT predate the transition to wrongness —\n  they *are* it. Fix site and cause site coincide here, so no carrier walk was needed\n  (checked `git log --oneline -- transition.py`: the only later touch is `695cab7b0c`,\n  a CLI-grammar refactor that does not touch the table).\n\nI considered and rejected task **9788-69c5-1972-4537** (jaggy-quasar-remit, the original\ncascade): its AC set says \"Other transitions (close, reopen, etc.) are unaffected\" and\nits I4a text stated only the open-parent form, so the missing behaviour does not fall\nunder its criteria. a392 is the ticket that generalised the invariant to cover this\nstate while enumerating only two of the three edges into it — a `plan_defect`, and the\none place a reviewer could plausibly have caught it. Noting for the record that a392's\nplan was reviewed and signed with an explicit \"only the closed -> open edge is added\"\nscope statement: the deferral was deliberate, and the escape is that the *documentation*\nit shipped was generalised past the *code* it shipped.\n\nPhase 2 (RED -> GREEN with a held-out oracle) follows."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787308398195465001,"uuid":"cbdc2d14-371b-4cd0-a8d4-8aa1258c57bd"}